# Recursive Functions in C

A function that calls itself is called a **recursive function**.

---

## 1. Basic Recursion with `static`

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    static int i;          // i = 0 (default)
    printf("Hello...\n");
    i++;
    if (i < 3)
        main();            // recursive main function
    return 0;
}
```

**Output**
```
Hello...
Hello...
Hello...
```

> Without a stopping condition (`if`), it keeps printing until the 8 MB stack is full, then gives a **segmentation fault**.

---

## 2. Pending Statements After the Recursive Call

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    static int i;
    if (i < 3)
    {
        printf("Hello..%d\n", i);
        i++;
        main();
    }
    printf("Hai...%d\n", i);
    return 0;
}
```

**Output**
```
Hello..0
Hello..1
Hello..2
Hai..3
Hai..3
Hai..3
Hai..3
```

**Why:** `i` lives in the data section (not the stack), so all calls share it. Three calls print `Hello`, the 4th call (`i = 3`) fails the `if` and prints `Hai..3`. The 3 pending `Hai` statements in the stack then run as each call returns.

| Stack frame | Condition | Prints | Pending |
|---|---|---|---|
| main (1) | `if (0<3)` ✔ | Hello..0 | Hai.. |
| main (2) | `if (1<3)` ✔ | Hello..1 | Hai.. |
| main (3) | `if (2<3)` ✔ | Hello..2 | Hai.. |
| main (4) | `if (3<3)` ✘ | Hai..3   | none  |

